Request an IDR frame when the FEC queue reports a loss without RFI

Carried from upstream https://github.com/moonlight-stream/moonlight-common-c/pull/147
(open as of 2026-08-12). Drop this patch and bump the submodule once it merges.

Without it, a frame the video FEC queue declares unrecoverable freezes the picture for
CONSECUTIVE_DROP_LIMIT frames - 120, so 2 seconds at 60 FPS and 4 at 30 - on any client
running without reference frame invalidation. This fork is exactly such a client on the
Homatics Box R 4K: decoderSupportsRefFrameInvalidationHevc() withholds RFI from
unconfirmed Amlogic decoders because they artifact and hang after packet loss, so the
Amlogic boxes take this path on every stream while the Shield TV never does.

The trace through the pinned tree, all in src/VideoDepacketizer.c:

  :80        strictIdrFrameWait = !isReferenceFrameInvalidationEnabled(), so true for us
  :103-113   dropFrameState() therefore always sets waitingForIdrFrame
  :1132      notifyFrameLost() only acts inside "if (!waitingForIdrFrame)", never taken
  :821       waitingForNextSuccessfulFrame is set only on processRtpPayload()'s frame-gap
             path, never on the FEC queue path that called notifyFrameLost()
  :1086      the next fully received frame requests an IDR only if that flag is set
  :119-129   so nothing recovers the stream until the consecutive drop limit trips

notifyFrameLost() exists specifically to speed recovery up ("This lets us avoid having to
wait until the next received frame"). In the non-RFI case it was an order of magnitude
slower than the frame-gap path it was meant to improve on.

Setting the flag is safe against the LC_ASSERT(!waitingForNextSuccessfulFrame) at :1102:
that line is reachable only when both wait flags are clear, and waitingForIdrFrame is
cleared only at :681-685, which clears waitingForNextSuccessfulFrame in the same block.
Speculative losses (RtpVideoQueue.c:219) set it too, which is correct - dropFrameState()
has already run unconditionally by then, so the frame state is gone either way.

diff --git a/src/VideoDepacketizer.c b/src/VideoDepacketizer.c
index 43f2df92..3e90c904 100644
--- a/src/VideoDepacketizer.c
+++ b/src/VideoDepacketizer.c
@@ -1153,6 +1153,11 @@ void notifyFrameLost(unsigned int frameNumber, bool speculative) {
         // Notify the host that we lost this one
         connectionDetectedFrameLoss(startFrameNumber, frameNumber);
     }
+    else {
+        // This loss came from the FEC queue, not processRtpPayload()'s frame gap detection,
+        // so waitingForNextSuccessfulFrame was never set and we'd never request the IDR frame.
+        waitingForNextSuccessfulFrame = true;
+    }
 }

 // Add an RTP Packet to the queue
